---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const vocabCfg = {
  curves: [{ expr: 'vmax*x/(x + k)', label: 'words you actually know', color: '#3b82f6', width: 2.4 }],
  params: [
    { name: 'vmax', label: 'target vocabulary', min: 500, max: 5000, step: 100, value: 3000, unit: 'words' },
    { name: 'k', label: 'how fast early gains come', min: 20, max: 300, step: 10, value: 120, unit: 'hours' },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 500, label: 'hours of practice' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 340,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/languages/foundational/how-to-learn-any-language">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Languages are not memorised — they are <strong>absorbed through meaningful contact</strong>,
    then cemented by use. The method that underlies nearly every successful learner: massive
    <strong>comprehensible input</strong> (listening/reading just past your level — Stephen
    Krashen's "i+1"), <strong>spaced repetition</strong> for the words that must stick, and
    <strong>early speaking</strong> to force retrieval. Talent is mostly hours, arranged well.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="vocabulary versus hours of practice — early words come fast; the plateau is where methods matter" widget="plotter" config={vocabCfg} />

  <p>
    The curve is honest and a little cruel: the first few hundred hours buy a huge share of
    everyday communication (the most frequent 1,000 words cover the bulk of ordinary speech),
    then gains slow as you chase rarer words. The plateau is where most learners quit — and
    where knowing <em>why</em> it exists keeps you going.
  </p>

  <h2>The working method</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Front-load frequency.</strong> Learn the top 1,000 words of your target language before grammar tables; they unlock the most real text and speech.</li>
    <li><strong>Use spaced repetition (SRS)</strong> for vocabulary — flashcards scheduled by forgetting curve (the psychology lesson on this atlas explains the maths).</li>
    <li><strong>Consume daily, slightly too hard.</strong> Podcasts, shows with subtitles, graded readers — content you understand ~80–90% of. That gap is the learning zone.</li>
    <li><strong>Speak before you're ready.</strong> Output forces retrieval; mistakes with feedback are the fastest teachers available.</li>
    <li><strong>Learn the script properly</strong> if there is one — Devanagari, Kana, Hangul — using stroke order and mnemonics. Reading is the highest-bandwidth input channel.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Levels you can aim at</h2>
  <p>
    The CEFR scale (A1 → C2) describes what you can <em>do</em>: A1 survive, B1 connect, C1 work
    fluently, C2 near-native nuance. Roughly: A2 ≈ 200–400 hours for an English speaker on a
    "close" language (Spanish), far more for Japanese — hours vary by language distance, methods
    vary by person, but the curve's shape is the same for everyone.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Krashen, Principles and Practice in Second Language Acquisition; CEFR (Council of Europe); Nation, Learning Vocabulary in Another Language.</p>
</TopicPage>
